  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x-2y?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-1),(2,-1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=(-2)-2(-1)=0, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=(2)-2(-1)=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=(2)-2(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044888/assets/6a2d6f3812df2844171dacf1675d8e3d90167010cb2ffcf939f88d9cfcafe8a9.png)",
